1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:03,861
October 29th, 2026.

2
00:00:03,881 --> 00:00:06,136
8:05 PM, UTC.

3
00:00:06,414 --> 00:00:09,562
On ten blockchains, within
minutes, money starts leaving

4
00:00:09,582 --> 00:00:12,969
Binance — the biggest crypto
exchange on Earth.

5
00:00:13,683 --> 00:00:15,081
Not thousands.

6
00:00:15,101 --> 00:00:16,536
Not millions.

7
00:00:16,556 --> 00:00:17,651
Billions.

8
00:00:18,432 --> 00:00:22,338
Within about twenty minutes, most
of an estimated six point three

9
00:00:22,358 --> 00:00:24,017
billion dollars is gone.

10
00:00:24,528 --> 00:00:26,325
Nobody cracked a safe.

11
00:00:26,345 --> 00:00:29,778
Binance says it has found no
evidence that a single private key

12
00:00:29,798 --> 00:00:30,855
was leaked.

13
00:00:31,289 --> 00:00:34,737
Instead, its first investigation
pointed somewhere far more

14
00:00:34,757 --> 00:00:35,824
ordinary…

15
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:38,023
an engineer's laptop.

16
00:00:38,848 --> 00:00:42,423
This is the story of the biggest
crypto heist ever recorded

17
00:00:42,443 --> 00:00:45,830
— how the attackers got in, how
they emptied the wallets,

18
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:47,873
the race to freeze the money…

19
00:00:48,713 --> 00:00:51,423
and the one question nobody can
answer yet.

20
00:00:58,808 --> 00:01:02,693
To understand the size of this,
you need to understand Binance.

21
00:01:03,074 --> 00:01:05,655
It's the world's largest crypto
exchange,

22
00:01:05,675 --> 00:01:09,604
with around 335 million registered
users.

23
00:01:10,101 --> 00:01:16,406
In its 2025 annual report, it said
it held about 162.8 billion

24
00:01:16,426 --> 00:01:18,651
dollars in assets under custody.

25
00:01:19,425 --> 00:01:22,660
An exchange doesn't keep all of
that in one place.

26
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:24,055
Think of a store.

27
00:01:24,075 --> 00:01:27,162
Most of the money sits in the
vault — cold wallets,

28
00:01:27,182 --> 00:01:29,820
kept offline, slow and hard to
reach.

29
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:33,059
The rest stays in the cash
register — hot wallets

30
00:01:33,079 --> 00:01:36,444
— online, so people can withdraw
at any second.

31
00:01:36,975 --> 00:01:39,258
The cash register is convenient.

32
00:01:39,278 --> 00:01:42,413
It's also the part of the building
that faces the street.

33
00:01:42,771 --> 00:01:45,946
And Binance already knew what
happens when someone reaches into

34
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:46,546
it.

35
00:01:46,987 --> 00:01:48,827
Because it had happened before.

36
00:01:52,639 --> 00:01:54,692
May 7th, 2019.

37
00:01:54,712 --> 00:01:56,797
17:15 UTC.

38
00:01:57,063 --> 00:02:00,700
One transaction pulls seven
thousand Bitcoin out of Binance's

39
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,678
hot wallet — about forty million
dollars at the time.

40
00:02:05,309 --> 00:02:09,887
Binance later explained: hackers
had collected user API keys,

41
00:02:09,907 --> 00:02:13,350
two-factor codes and other
information — through phishing,

42
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:15,329
viruses and other attacks.

43
00:02:16,087 --> 00:02:17,350
Then they waited.

44
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:20,259
Binance said the hackers "had the
patience to wait"

45
00:02:20,279 --> 00:02:23,069
— striking through seemingly
unrelated accounts,

46
00:02:23,089 --> 00:02:26,620
with a withdrawal built to pass
every security check.

47
00:02:27,423 --> 00:02:30,488
The alarms fired only after the
money was gone.

48
00:02:30,847 --> 00:02:34,497
In a livestream, CEO Changpeng
Zhao — CZ

49
00:02:34,517 --> 00:02:38,712
— said Binance was considering an
idea first raised by a Bitcoin

50
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:42,505
developer: paying miners to
rewrite the blockchain and undo

51
00:02:42,525 --> 00:02:43,472
the theft.

52
00:02:44,362 --> 00:02:46,056
The backlash was immediate.

53
00:02:46,076 --> 00:02:49,899
Within a day, CZ dropped it,
saying the Bitcoin ledger is "the

54
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:52,531
most immutable ledger on the
planet."

55
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:57,541
Binance covered the loss from SAFU
— its Secure Asset Fund for Users,

56
00:02:57,561 --> 00:03:01,461
funded since 2018 with ten percent
of trading fees.

57
00:03:02,086 --> 00:03:04,439
A week later, withdrawals were
back.

58
00:03:04,459 --> 00:03:09,467
In 2022, an attacker abused a
bridge bug on Binance's own BNB

59
00:03:09,487 --> 00:03:12,985
Chain to create two million BNB
out of thin air

60
00:03:13,005 --> 00:03:15,254
— and the chain was halted.

61
00:03:16,134 --> 00:03:18,291
Each time, the lesson was the
same.

62
00:03:18,311 --> 00:03:20,367
The weak point is never the math.

63
00:03:20,387 --> 00:03:22,062
It's everything around it.

64
00:03:25,825 --> 00:03:28,350
Now — back to October 2026.

65
00:03:28,641 --> 00:03:32,298
Binance's initial investigation
suggested the incident may have

66
00:03:32,318 --> 00:03:36,797
started with a compromised laptop
belonging to a DevOps engineer

67
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:40,749
— one of the people who build and
run the machinery behind the

68
00:03:40,769 --> 00:03:41,816
exchange.

69
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:46,235
From that laptop, an attacker
reached what Binance calls a

70
00:03:46,255 --> 00:03:50,005
"service container" — part of its
internal infrastructure

71
00:03:50,025 --> 00:03:52,840
— and from there, submitted
unauthorized withdrawal

72
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:54,005
transactions.

73
00:03:54,844 --> 00:03:58,769
Hours later, Binance said it had
found additional points of breach.

74
00:03:59,063 --> 00:04:03,286
Co-CEO Richard Teng put it
bluntly: this was not a single

75
00:04:03,306 --> 00:04:04,643
"one-click" action.

76
00:04:05,026 --> 00:04:08,786
The attacker did not simply use
one engineer's permissions.

77
00:04:09,112 --> 00:04:10,500
And it was planned.

78
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:14,048
According to blockchain analysts
at TRM Labs,

79
00:04:14,068 --> 00:04:17,036
staging began at least thirty-six
hours earlier,

80
00:04:17,056 --> 00:04:20,929
with small withdrawals from the
crypto mixer Tornado Cash funding

81
00:04:20,949 --> 00:04:23,070
the attacker's operating wallet.

82
00:04:24,021 --> 00:04:27,270
Investigators believe the
attackers didn't need to steal the

83
00:04:27,290 --> 00:04:28,026
keys.

84
00:04:28,299 --> 00:04:31,254
They got Binance's own systems to
sign for them.

85
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:34,925
So how do you turn a laptop into
six billion dollars?

86
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,110
You give yourself twenty minutes.

87
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:42,544
8:05 PM.

88
00:04:42,564 --> 00:04:46,048
The first funds start moving — on
the XRP Ledger,

89
00:04:46,068 --> 00:04:50,393
where analysts count about 296
million dollars drained in the

90
00:04:50,413 --> 00:04:51,894
first four minutes.

91
00:04:52,618 --> 00:04:55,117
Ten networks follow — Bitcoin,
Ethereum,

92
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:56,958
Tron, Solana, and more.

93
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:59,840
On nine of them, it looks almost
normal:

94
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:03,375
ordinary transfers, signed by
Binance's own wallets.

95
00:05:03,845 --> 00:05:05,633
Ethereum is different.

96
00:05:05,653 --> 00:05:09,932
At 8:20, one transaction targets a
Binance hot wallet labeled

97
00:05:09,952 --> 00:05:11,373
"Binance 14."

98
00:05:11,852 --> 00:05:16,337
Inside it: fifty authorizations —
all signed with Binance 14's own

99
00:05:16,357 --> 00:05:21,034
key — using an Ethereum feature
called EIP-7702,

100
00:05:21,054 --> 00:05:25,022
which lets a wallet hand its
powers to a piece of code.

101
00:05:25,904 --> 00:05:27,113
Why fifty?

102
00:05:27,133 --> 00:05:30,441
Every wallet keeps a running count
of its transactions.

103
00:05:30,461 --> 00:05:33,663
An authorization only works if it
matches the right number

104
00:05:33,683 --> 00:05:37,866
— and the attacker didn't know
which number Binance 14 was on.

105
00:05:38,390 --> 00:05:40,792
So they brought fifty signed
permission slips,

106
00:05:40,812 --> 00:05:42,875
one for every possible number.

107
00:05:43,306 --> 00:05:44,961
Exactly one matched.

108
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:49,570
In that moment, Binance 14 stopped
being Binance's wallet.

109
00:05:49,884 --> 00:05:51,999
It was running the attacker's
sweeper code.

110
00:05:52,278 --> 00:05:57,870
In under four minutes, it emptied
— about 167,000 ETH,

111
00:05:57,890 --> 00:06:02,873
roughly 1.6 billion USDT, and
dozens of other tokens.

112
00:06:03,665 --> 00:06:08,914
By TRM's count, Ethereum alone
accounted for about 3.8 billion

113
00:06:08,934 --> 00:06:09,795
dollars.

114
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:16,815
On Bitcoin: 18,270 BTC — around
1.47 billion more.

115
00:06:17,439 --> 00:06:19,894
Most of it was gone in about
twenty minutes.

116
00:06:20,164 --> 00:06:23,034
And then, around 11 PM, a second
wave hit

117
00:06:23,054 --> 00:06:25,099
— on Avalanche and Polygon.

118
00:06:29,111 --> 00:06:31,231
While the money moved, a race
began.

119
00:06:31,506 --> 00:06:33,516
Some crypto can be frozen.

120
00:06:33,536 --> 00:06:39,262
Stablecoins like USDT and USDC are
issued by companies that can lock

121
00:06:39,282 --> 00:06:41,526
tokens in a specific wallet.

122
00:06:42,266 --> 00:06:49,364
At 9:01 PM, Tether froze about 971
million USDT in the attacker's

123
00:06:49,384 --> 00:06:51,221
main receiving wallet.

124
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:56,748
Three minutes later, Circle froze
823 million USDC.

125
00:06:57,193 --> 00:07:00,091
Soon after, Binance posted for the
first time:

126
00:07:00,111 --> 00:07:03,063
it was aware of "a potential
security issue."

127
00:07:03,496 --> 00:07:08,898
Tether's CEO said more than 1.5
billion dollars in USDT was

128
00:07:08,918 --> 00:07:09,856
frozen.

129
00:07:10,298 --> 00:07:14,378
Ripple and World Liberty Financial
froze their stablecoins too.

130
00:07:14,751 --> 00:07:18,161
That night, Binance suspended
withdrawals and promised to cover

131
00:07:18,181 --> 00:07:19,176
every loss.

132
00:07:19,472 --> 00:07:23,699
Just after midnight UTC,
Arbitrum's Security Council moved

133
00:07:23,719 --> 00:07:28,317
about 30 million dollars in ETH
and ARB out of the attacker's

134
00:07:28,337 --> 00:07:29,147
reach.

135
00:07:29,889 --> 00:07:34,804
Binance now says about 2.85
billion dollars has been frozen or

136
00:07:34,824 --> 00:07:35,944
intercepted.

137
00:07:36,389 --> 00:07:41,624
Elliptic counts 2.84 billion; TRM,
around 2.7.

138
00:07:42,106 --> 00:07:45,641
But Bitcoin and Ether have no
company that can freeze them.

139
00:07:45,893 --> 00:07:51,437
And the attacker kept moving —
selling about 325 million USDT to

140
00:07:51,457 --> 00:07:54,770
market makers at more than a ten
percent discount,

141
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:58,258
and borrowing ether against USDT
on Aave.

142
00:07:59,116 --> 00:08:03,026
As of TRM's latest analysis, the
attacker still holds more than

143
00:08:03,046 --> 00:08:11,418
420,000 ETH and 18,270 BTC — about
2.4 billion dollars beyond any

144
00:08:11,438 --> 00:08:14,712
freeze — the Bitcoin splitting
into smaller and smaller

145
00:08:14,732 --> 00:08:15,706
transfers.

146
00:08:19,903 --> 00:08:22,818
By the next morning, investigators
had a name.

147
00:08:23,198 --> 00:08:26,705
TRM Labs attributed the attack
"with high confidence" to

148
00:08:26,725 --> 00:08:30,615
TraderTraitor — a North Korean
state-backed hacking unit,

149
00:08:30,635 --> 00:08:33,898
widely described as part of the
Lazarus Group.

150
00:08:34,768 --> 00:08:37,290
Chainalysis reached the same
conclusion,

151
00:08:37,310 --> 00:08:40,794
after seeing funds from
September's Bitget hack mixed with

152
00:08:40,814 --> 00:08:42,948
funds stolen from Binance.

153
00:08:43,485 --> 00:08:44,899
Elliptic agreed.

154
00:08:44,919 --> 00:08:49,279
Then the FBI said it publicly:
North Korea was responsible.

155
00:08:49,650 --> 00:08:51,402
North Korea denies it.

156
00:08:51,422 --> 00:08:54,999
In a statement carried by state
media, Kim Yo Jong

157
00:08:55,019 --> 00:08:59,472
— Kim Jong Un's sister — called
the accusations "absurd rhetoric."

158
00:09:00,091 --> 00:09:03,894
Binance says it's working with law
enforcement to confirm who the

159
00:09:03,914 --> 00:09:04,991
attackers are.

160
00:09:05,435 --> 00:09:07,970
If TraderTraitor sounds familiar,
it should.

161
00:09:08,237 --> 00:09:13,353
In February 2025, the FBI blamed
the same group for the hack of

162
00:09:13,373 --> 00:09:18,007
Bybit — about 1.5 billion dollars,
the record until now.

163
00:09:18,724 --> 00:09:22,277
That breach was traced to a
compromised developer machine at

164
00:09:22,297 --> 00:09:25,259
Safe, the wallet software Bybit
relied on.

165
00:09:25,828 --> 00:09:31,733
In 2024, Japan's DMM Bitcoin lost
about 300 million dollars.

166
00:09:32,126 --> 00:09:35,203
According to the FBI and Japanese
police,

167
00:09:35,223 --> 00:09:39,047
it began when a fake recruiter
sent an employee at a partner

168
00:09:39,067 --> 00:09:41,816
company a malicious
"pre-employment test."

169
00:09:42,588 --> 00:09:44,137
Fake job offers.

170
00:09:44,157 --> 00:09:45,517
Poisoned code.

171
00:09:45,537 --> 00:09:47,940
Trusted machines turned into
doorways.

172
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:51,680
It's a playbook security agencies
have warned about for years.

173
00:09:52,056 --> 00:09:53,858
Remember that first one.

174
00:09:53,878 --> 00:09:55,540
We'll come back to it.

175
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:59,867
TRM says this attack fits the
pattern — staging well before the

176
00:09:59,887 --> 00:10:04,340
theft, Tornado Cash funding, and
signatures collected in advance…

177
00:10:05,129 --> 00:10:06,934
then fired in one burst.

178
00:10:10,712 --> 00:10:12,212
Markets felt it.

179
00:10:12,232 --> 00:10:17,222
Bitcoin slid to a six-week low
near 76,400 dollars.

180
00:10:17,573 --> 00:10:19,378
Ether hit a two-month low.

181
00:10:19,398 --> 00:10:23,883
BNB fell about five percent, and
shares of Coinbase and Strategy

182
00:10:23,903 --> 00:10:26,123
dropped in after-hours trading.

183
00:10:26,694 --> 00:10:31,239
Binance's answer echoed 2019:
customers won't pay for this.

184
00:10:31,567 --> 00:10:36,338
It said it had set aside about
6.34 billion dollars to cover the

185
00:10:36,358 --> 00:10:39,783
losses — far more than its SAFU
emergency fund,

186
00:10:39,803 --> 00:10:42,792
which reportedly holds around one
billion.

187
00:10:43,643 --> 00:10:48,144
Co-CEO Richard Teng wrote, "Our
users will not bear the financial

188
00:10:48,164 --> 00:10:50,093
impact of this incident."

189
00:10:50,574 --> 00:10:54,583
Founder CZ — who stepped down as
CEO in 2023

190
00:10:54,603 --> 00:10:58,516
— said that as a shareholder, he
expected every user to be made

191
00:10:58,536 --> 00:10:59,264
whole.

192
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:03,591
On Sunday, November 1st,
withdrawals started coming back in

193
00:11:03,611 --> 00:11:05,490
phases — Bitcoin first.

194
00:11:05,844 --> 00:11:11,073
That day, users pulled out about
4.65 billion dollars and deposited

195
00:11:11,093 --> 00:11:13,654
about 1.01 billion.

196
00:11:14,341 --> 00:11:20,290
Net outflow: 3.64 billion — which
Binance says is 2.1 percent of its

197
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:21,571
total assets.

198
00:11:21,997 --> 00:11:25,457
CZ's takeaway: "98% choose to
stay."

199
00:11:25,815 --> 00:11:29,746
By then, Binance said it had
replenished about 95 percent of

200
00:11:29,766 --> 00:11:33,175
the affected assets — and was
still buying the rest.

201
00:11:37,296 --> 00:11:41,021
And yet, the biggest questions are
only starting to get answers.

202
00:11:41,392 --> 00:11:43,132
How did the attackers get in?

203
00:11:43,408 --> 00:11:46,655
On November 3rd, Binance gave its
first answer.

204
00:11:46,675 --> 00:11:50,469
In September, a recruiter on a job
platform approached one of its

205
00:11:50,489 --> 00:11:51,490
engineers.

206
00:11:51,913 --> 00:11:54,153
There was a video interview with
"HR"…

207
00:11:54,578 --> 00:11:58,258
then one with the "CEO" of a
company that didn't exist…

208
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:01,455
and finally, a take-home coding
assignment.

209
00:12:01,719 --> 00:12:03,391
The other points of breach?

210
00:12:03,411 --> 00:12:06,600
Binance says the attackers used
that engineer's computer

211
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:10,494
— and an internal AI tool — to
reach colleagues' machines,

212
00:12:10,514 --> 00:12:14,580
until they found a token that let
them deploy code into the wallet

213
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:15,456
system.

214
00:12:16,375 --> 00:12:19,179
Exactly how that became fifty
valid signatures

215
00:12:19,199 --> 00:12:22,758
— with no evidence, Binance says,
of a leaked private key

216
00:12:22,778 --> 00:12:26,310
— will have to wait for the full
technical report.

217
00:12:27,166 --> 00:12:30,709
And the billions in Bitcoin and
Ether that no issuer can freeze

218
00:12:30,729 --> 00:12:33,041
— will any of it ever come back?

219
00:12:33,539 --> 00:12:38,024
In 2019, hackers phished their way
into user accounts and took forty

220
00:12:38,044 --> 00:12:39,334
million dollars.

221
00:12:39,843 --> 00:12:44,407
In 2026, investigators believe the
attackers turned the exchange's

222
00:12:44,427 --> 00:12:48,433
own machinery against it — and
moved more than a hundred and

223
00:12:48,453 --> 00:12:50,138
fifty times as much.

224
00:12:51,493 --> 00:12:53,433
The money moved in twenty minutes.

225
00:12:53,690 --> 00:12:55,990
The investigation could take
years.

226
00:12:58,745 --> 00:13:00,720
So what does this mean for you?

227
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:04,678
Any money you keep on an exchange
is protected by their security

228
00:13:04,698 --> 00:13:05,950
— not yours.

229
00:13:06,297 --> 00:13:09,938
Use the strongest two-factor you
can, like a hardware key or a

230
00:13:09,958 --> 00:13:10,727
passkey.

231
00:13:11,069 --> 00:13:13,415
Turn on withdrawal address
whitelists.

232
00:13:13,435 --> 00:13:16,370
Don't keep more on an exchange
than you need to trade.

233
00:13:16,758 --> 00:13:20,829
And if you work in tech or crypto:
treat surprise job offers and

234
00:13:20,849 --> 00:13:24,738
take-home "coding tests" from
strangers like a live wire.

235
00:13:25,310 --> 00:13:28,419
Investigators have seen that door
used again and again

236
00:13:28,439 --> 00:13:31,415
— and Binance says it was used
this time.

237
00:13:31,977 --> 00:13:33,696
This story isn't over.

238
00:13:33,716 --> 00:13:37,246
Subscribe so you don't miss what
the investigation finds next

239
00:13:37,266 --> 00:13:41,207
— and tell me in the comments:
should exchanges keep billions in

240
00:13:41,227 --> 00:13:43,356
wallets that are online at all?
